{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-12_09-55-08-hashicorp_raft-real-run && python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport json\nd=json.load(open('research.json'))\nprint(json.dumps(d['investigations'],indent=1)[:3000])\nprint(\\\"--- KNOWLEDGE ---\\\")\nprint(json.dumps(d['knowledge'],indent=1)[:1500])\nEOF\"","aggregated_output":"[\n {\n  \"id\": \"c4f67dac947d44fe86d65b317fd5d5b4-question-0\",\n  \"version\": 1,\n  \"question\": \"After a follower successfully installs an InstallSnapshot whose last included entry is (index L, term T), must the follower accept the leader's next AppendEntries with PrevLogEntry=L and PrevLogTerm=T, and does it still do so when the follower already held a log entry at L from an earlier term?\",\n  \"hypothesis\": \"installSnapshot sets lastApplied and lastSnapshot but never calls setLastLog nor invalidates the pre-existing log entry at L. getLastEntry returns (lastLogIndex,lastLogTerm) whenever lastLogIndex >= lastSnapshotIndex, so once the snapshot makes lastSnapshotIndex == L while the stale log entry keeps lastLogIndex == L, the tie is resolved in favour of the stale log term. The follower's PrevLogEntry==L check then compares the leader's snapshot term T against the stale term and answers Success=false with NoRetryBackoff=true. The leader sets nextIndex = max(min(nextIndex-1, resp.LastLog+1), 1), which lands on L; setPreviousLog(L) then needs the leader's own log entry L-1, which is absent once the leader has compacted up to its snapshot index (compactLogsWithTrailing can leave firstIndex > L-1), so replicateTo jumps to SEND_SNAP, re-sends the same snapshot, restores nextIndex=L+1, and the same rejection recurs.\",\n  \"discriminator\": \"Drive one node with real RPCs: bootstrap it as a Nonvoter, seed a command at index 2 with an AppendEntries whose Term is 2, install an InstallSnapshot with LastLogIndex=2 and LastLogTerm=3, then send AppendEntries with PrevLogEntry=2/PrevLogTerm=3. The necessary property is met only if the last append succeeds; the suspicion holds if it fails while an otherwise identical control node without the seeded entry succeeds. Refuted if the post-snapshot append succeeds in both cases, or if getLastEntry reports the snapshot term for index 2.\",\n  \"disposition\": \"active\",\n  \"parent_id\": null,\n  \"source_ids\": [\n   \"src-installsnap\",\n   \"src-lastindex\",\n   \"src-appendentries\",\n   \"src-compact\",\n   \"src-replication\",\n   \"src-repl-snap\"\n  ],\n  \"counterevidence\": [],\n  \"limitations\": [],\n  \"resume_conditions\": [],\n  \"knowledge\": [],\n  \"open_gaps\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"c4f67dac947d44fe86d65b317fd5d5b4-gap-0\",\n    \"question\": \"Does the leader side actually restore the blocking state in one execution: with a leader whose log has been compacted so that the snapshot index L is at its head, does an unrepairable follower produce repeated InstallSnapshot attempts with no committed progress rather than converging after one snapshot?\",\n    \"experiment_id\": null,\n    \"operation_id\": \"c4f67dac947d44fe86d65b317fd5d5b4\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"latest_execution\": {\n   \"id\": \"72367650c8d0470d8197a7d2c0f97ce1\",\n   \"experiment_id\": \"c4f67dac947d44fe86d65b317fd5d5b4-experiment\",\n   \"status\": \"completed\",\n   \"exit_code\": 0,\n   \"record\": \"logs/72367650c8d0470d8197a7d2c0f97ce1/check.json\",\n   \"observations\": \"logs/72367650c8d0470d8197a7d2c0f97ce1/observations.json\"\n  },\n  \"la\n--- KNOWLEDGE ---\n[\n {\n  \"id\": \"c4f67dac947d44fe86d65b317fd5d5b4-knowledge-0\",\n  \"revision\": 1,\n  \"kind\": \"behavior\",\n  \"statement\": \"On accepting an InstallSnapshot, installSnapshot sets lastApplied=req.LastLogIndex and lastSnapshot=(req.LastLogIndex,req.LastLogTerm), restores latest and committed configuration from the snapshot payload, then clears the log store (removeOldLogs) for a monotonic store or compacts it (compactLogs(req.LastLogIndex)) otherwise, and finally sets resp.Success=true. It never calls setLastLog and never truncates a pre-existing log suffix at or above req.LastLogIndex, so lastLogIndex keeps its pre-snapshot value and a stale entry at the snapshot index can remain in the store.\",\n  \"conditions\": \"Reached only when the RPC header passes checkRPCHeader, the snapshot version is supported, req.Term >= currentTerm, the snapshot bytes are fully received (n == req.Size) and the FSM restore succeeds.\",\n  \"source_ids\": [\n   \"src-installsnap\",\n   \"src-compact\"\n  ],\n  \"executions\": {},\n  \"operation_id\": \"c4f67dac947d44fe86d65b317fd5d5b4\",\n  \"uses\": [],\n  \"last_correction\": null\n },\n {\n  \"id\": \"c4f67dac947d44fe86d65b317fd5d5b4-knowledge-1\",\n  \"revision\": 1,\n  \"kind\": \"behavior\",\n  \"statement\": \"raftState.getLastIndex returns max(lastLogIndex,lastSnapshotIndex) and getLastEntry returns (lastLogIndex,lastLogTerm) whenever lastLogIndex >= lastSnapshotIndex, otherwise (lastSnapshotIndex,lastSnapshotTerm). The comparison is on index only, so when the two sources name the same index with\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
